ourth shrine in  massachusetts in the story each of the  three so-called miracles were followed  up by tragedy which was true in the case  of the three portuguese children who all  died in the next one to two years during  the 1918 flu pandemic ironically the  release of this movie would also be  hampered by a major pandemic to learn  some of the secrets of the dark fates  behind this event once heralded as a  modern day miracle  stick around to the end of this video  the unholy tells the story of this small  church town near boston being put on the  global map due to the supposed miracle  that takes place there when a deaf and  mute girl named alice is gifted with the  ability to hear and speak perfectly but  one journalist named jerry fenn begins  to suspect that the entity performing  these supposed miracles is anything but  a holy one  hence the title of the movie jerry was  once a prominent reporter but his desire  for fame pushed him to fabricate stories  leading to him being shunned by any  respectable publication we see he is now  working for a site called bizarre bean  town  beantown is a reference to the city of  boston once known for its baked beans  but jerry is sent to the countryside in  massachusetts to investigate his story  a small town by the name of banfield his  story turns out to be a dud  but when he happens across alice he  realizes he's got a much bigger scoop on  his hands before eventually discovering  that he may have put himself and many  others in grave grave danger  i've got a few things you'll want to  look out for when watching this movie so  let's get into them this video is  sponsored by sony pictures home  entertainment  [Music]  when jerry first arrives in banfield he  comes across this doll known as a kern  baby which is said to be planted there  by farmers as a token of good luck  however this particular one is labeled  with an impossible date  february 31st 1945 which is said to be  part of a practice to contain bad  spirits little did i know at the time  there are actually many stories of grave  markers and monuments with this date  while it can sometimes be used to denote  an unknown date many use it out of  superstition essentially the idea is  that the evil spirit will not be  released on any anniversary because the  date listed can never come  jerry shows his willingness to fudge a  story for money by breaking open the  kern baby  and making up a story about this  talisman being broken and an unholy evil  being released thirsty for blood  in this sense the story is kind of a  self-fulfilling prophecy and this is  where we start to see the signs that an  evil has indeed been unleashed but its  name is not virgin mary but rather  mary ellenor take the use of color for  example we all know from carrie's mom  that red is the color of the devil and  we see that reflected in alice who this  satanic entity speaks to and deceives  when we first lay eyes on her it's the  red tail lights of jerry's truck when  she gains her hearing back she undergoes  an auditory test which bathes her in red  light the water that mary elenor emerges  from in jerry's dream is  red and after she takes her first victim  this holy statue is illuminated in the  devil's color  these are all signs that what may appear  to be holy is actually a demonic trick  another example of this is the motel  that jerry stays in in banfield the sign  basks the entire area in red lights and  in more than one instance mary elenor  materializes from that direction  [Music]  you'll notice the sign is always  flickering and the letter m always  flicks independently of the other  letters could that possibly stand for  mary or  possibly miracle either way it draws my  attention and makes me think that  something isn't right here alice shows  that she's also able to help other  people get healed by mary when she helps  a crippled boy walk for the first time  and when she does something like this we  see one of these statues crying red  tears we see this happen a few times and  as a non-catholic i was curious about  what this may have signified so i looked  it up according to the site called the  conversation  mary cries not only over the sins of the  world but also over the pain she endured  in her earthly life  i think the meaning behind this imagery  in the movie is open to interpretation  these could be the real virgin mary's  tears and she's weeping for everyone who  is tricked into devoting themselves to a  false deity  or these could be mary ellenor's tears  for the pain that she endured in her  life which we see in the prologue i  think it's up to you to be on the  lookout and make the judgment as you  watch the film  another detail i found interesting was  alice's music preferences once she gains  the ability to hear she takes an  interest in country music but the song  we actually witness her listening to is  voices in my head by ashley tisdale  which must be connected to the way that  the false spirit communicates with her  where she's able to hear it but nobody  else is  one of the most interesting character  dynamics at play here is the  relationship between jerry and alice  jerry becomes the exclusive reporter who  has access to her and  in a strange way they both kind of  relate to each other alice is a girl who  is literally able to use her voice for  the first time and  jerry has in essence just gotten his  voice back or at least his journalistic  voice after being de-platformed by the  paper that he used to work for  this makes them both ideal victims for  mary elmore because they're both  desperate to speak freely  so despite this movie seeming to have a  bit of a message about just  being honest and humble in your work you  know there's still going to be some crew  reference easter eggs so here's a few of  them that you can look out for  during the news and social media montage  where word of alice's miracle is  spreading around the country there's an  instagram post from an account called  joshsg78 which was likely put there by  first assistant editor josh zigarlotta  later on there's a youtube live stream  of the ceremony at the new shrine on  sacramento tv  not to be confused with sakura tv from  death note there are only three people  active in the chat and they include  grace a reference to the vfx coordinator  and  nolan r the vfx editor i'm not sure who  ron bingo is but if anyone figures it  out drop me a comment  just taking a quick glance at some of  the ads for this movie i couldn't blame  you if you had the impression that it  was going to be somewhat similar to  2018's the nun  but outside of the generic concept of an  evil entity posing as a holy one it  really couldn't be much more different  instead of the expected haunted house  tropes the unholy is more of a character  piece with a dread that bubbles up  slowly until the big finale  [Applause]  you're not going to see bloody mary  popping up in the mirror for cheap  scares and it's kind of refreshing to  see a movie like this in a modern  setting with a touch of humor where  you have a son yeah  16 nice 15. you ever hear the band  metallica  i also mentioned the dream scene which  offers some surreal imagery that i liked  but what i was most impressed with was  the way that the film handles the  antagonist like the characters we kind  of slowly realize that mary ellenore is  not who she says she is but if you look  closely the clues are there  when alice first makes contact you can  see something that looks like a ghostly  figure fade before her which appears  again as a glitch in one of jerry's  videos  then when we actually do see the  creature who fittingly busts out of a  statue of the actual virgin mary  i won't spoil her appearance for those  who haven't seen it but it's like it's a  very own brand of creepy and i took a  liking to the way it looks the way it  moves and even the way it sounds it was  all very unique in a modern horror  landscape that already has  a lo